    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nGarbage collection automatically reclaims memory for objects that are no longer reachable. It does not make resource management unnecessary; files, sockets, and database connections still need explicit lifecycle handling.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you understand the design or language idea well enough to use it, not just repeat its definition. The interviewer can probe coupling, maintainability, runtime behavior, or language constraints.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Define the concept in one sentence.\n2. Explain the language or design rule behind it.\n3. Show the consequence with a small example.\n4. State the main trade-off or limitation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define the concept first, then explain why the language or design uses that model. I would give one concrete example and finish with the limitation I would keep in mind in production.\n\n**Example :**\nA short-lived request object can become unreachable after the request completes and later be reclaimed by the runtime.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nGC reduces manual memory errors but introduces runtime overhead and does not fix leaks caused by unintended references.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing a language feature with a design principle.\n- Giving a textbook definition without explaining the consequence in code.\n- Presenting a guideline as an absolute rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nGarbage collection automatically reclaims memory for objects that are no longer reachable. It does not make resource management unnecessary; files, sockets, and database connections still need explicit lifecycle handling.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you understand the design or language idea well enough to use it, not just repeat its definition. The interviewer can probe coupling, maintainability, runtime behavior, or language constraints.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Define the concept in one sentence.\n2. Explain the language or design rule behind it.\n3. Show the consequence with a small example.\n4. State the main trade-off or limitation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define the concept first, then explain why the language or design uses that model. I would give one concrete example and finish with the limitation I would keep in mind in production.\n\n**Example :**\nA short-lived request object can become unreachable after the request completes and later be reclaimed by the runtime.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nGC reduces manual memory errors but introduces runtime overhead and does not fix leaks caused by unintended references.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing a language feature with a design principle.\n- Giving a textbook definition without explaining the consequence in code.\n- Presenting a guideline as an absolute rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
